{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Six days you shall labor, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose HaShem commands laboring on six days. Also, what is His intention in saying <b>and you shall do</b>, etc. And it appears that He intends by way of the verse's statement in parashat Behar Sinai (Leviticus 25:20): ״וְכִי תֹאמְרוּ מַה נֹּאכַל בַּשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית״ etc. ״וְצִוִּיתִי״ etc. — and these are the matters stated here, according to what preceded, namely His saying ״זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת לְקַדְּשׁוֹ״, which contains no profane matters. If so, one who produces and eats day by day — what will he eat on the consecrated day? Therefore He said <b>six days</b> alone <b>you shall labor</b>, and in them you shall do <b>all your work</b> — meaning, what you need for the sixth and the seventh, by way of His saying there (ibid.) ״וְעָשָׂת אֶת הַתְּבוּאָה לִשցְלֹשׁ״ etc., for HaShem makes a promise to those who keep His commandments." "It further intends to command not to think about weekday labor on Shabbat, and that is His saying <b>six days</b>, etc. — and let it be in your eyes as though you have already done all the work you have to do. And this is by way of their saying, of blessed memory (Mekhilta): rest as though your work is already done; for otherwise one might think thoughts to prepare his labor and what is lacking from the work to do it on a weekday. Go out and learn from the deed of the pious man (Shabbat 150a) who penalized himself for having thought on Shabbat to repair a broken fence, so that he would not rebuild it again, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:04.500448725Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}